1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
This is verdict-agent. You give it a list of software products,
and it judges each one: real, alive and trustworthy, or not.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Every verdict comes with evidence links
a person can open and check.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
It looks at the home page, dead links, the help centre,
the Wayback Machine's first and last snapshots, and risk news.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Rules in a pure-function core turn that evidence
into Green, Yellow or Red, with a confidence.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,000
On the half of Stackgini's samples we didn't tune on, it gets 9 of 20 right.
It goes wrong mostly on Yellow products: it rates them Green, so it's too trusting.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Two authors built this through pull requests,
and Norma checked every one.

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Norma flagged a HIGH finding: an unbounded Promise.all
that checked every link on a vendor's site at once.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
That hammers the vendor. We replaced it with a limiter,
two requests at a time. The re-check came back clean.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
We kept one finding: console.log in eval.ts.
It's a command-line tool, and the printed table is its output.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Both authors accepted it independently,
for the same reason.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The clash: author one kept the fetch loops sequential on purpose,
so the one-second politeness gap per site would hold.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Author two needed to judge many products, so they moved politeness
into the fetcher, as a queue per site, and ran products in parallel.

13
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:42,000
We chose author two's way for the whole repo: politeness in one place
is safer than asking every caller to remember it.

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
We gave up the simpler one-request-at-a-time model.
And honestly, author two knew about author one's choice.

15
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Every finding and its outcome is in the Norma log.
No code was deleted to hide a finding. Thanks.
